---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { SITE, LICENCE } from '../lib/site';
---

<Page
  title="Licence"
  description="Everything posted on MathemAct is published under CC BY 4.0. This page explains what that means in practice."
  lead="All contributed content is CC BY 4.0. You can copy it, adapt it, and use it in any context, including commercial ones, provided you say where it came from."
  updated="August 2026"
>
  <h2>What is licensed</h2>

  <p>
    The corpus — reports, entries, comments, and claims — is contributed by its
    authors under the{' '}
    <a href={LICENCE.url} rel="license">{LICENCE.fullName}</a>. The licence covers the
    text content of submissions. It does not cover transcript excerpts that reproduce a
    third party's copyrighted output verbatim; the submitter is responsible for ensuring
    that excerpts they paste are ones they have the right to share.
  </p>

  <p>
    The site's own code is published under the{' '}
    <a href={`${SITE.repository}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>MIT Licence</a>.
    Design tokens, layout, and component code are all MIT. The IBM Plex font family is
    licensed under the SIL Open Font Licence 1.1.
  </p>

  <h2>What CC BY 4.0 requires</h2>

  <p>When you share or build on content from this site, CC BY 4.0 requires you to:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Credit the author.</strong> A display name and a link to the report page is
      sufficient. If the author uses a pseudonym, use that.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Link to the licence.</strong> A link to{' '}
      <a href={LICENCE.url}>{LICENCE.url}</a> or the text "CC BY 4.0" is enough.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Indicate changes.</strong> If you adapted the content, say so.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Commercial use is permitted. Creating derivative works is permitted. Relicensing your
    derivative under different terms is permitted, provided the original remains attributed
    and the CC BY 4.0 rights are passed on to recipients of the original.
  </p>

  <h2>The downloadable dataset</h2>

  <p>
    The nightly export at{' '}
    <a href={`${SITE.repository}/tree/main/data`}>{SITE.repository}/tree/main/data</a>{' '}
    contains the full published corpus in JSON and CSV, under the same CC BY 4.0 terms.
    Researchers using the dataset for analysis should cite the repository and note the date
    of the export they used; the manifest file records both.
  </p>

  <h2>What "attribution" means for the dataset</h2>

  <p>
    A paper or analysis that draws on the MathemAct corpus should cite it roughly as:
  </p>

  <blockquote class="attribution-example">
    <p>
      MathemAct corpus, retrieved [date] from{' '}
      <a href={SITE.repository}>{SITE.repository}</a>. Licensed under CC BY 4.0.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    Each report page gives its own URL if you need to cite a specific account. Individual
    reports were contributed by the authors named on their pages.
  </p>

  <h2>Institution data</h2>

  <p>
    Institutional affiliations are derived from the{' '}
    <a href="https://ror.org/">Research Organization Registry (ROR)</a>, which publishes its
    data under CC0 — no restrictions apply to its use. The ROR data is not included in the
    MathemAct export; only the derived institution name and country fields appear there.
  </p>
</Page>

<style>
  .attribution-example {
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    border-inline-start: var(--border-width-thick) solid var(--border-color);
    font-style: normal;
  }

  .attribution-example p {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
